00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:03.669 align:start position:0%
if sharing is caring then a handful of  states are betting that the sharing of  salary details is a step towards evening  the playing field among workers  right now the data highlights a  persistent wage gap across gender and  race  white women who work full-time take home  83 cents for every dollar that men earn  for some women of color the gap is wider  71 cents for black women and 64 cents  for hispanic women  so what are states doing colorado is  leading the way it's 2021 pay  transparency law requires employers to  publish the salary range and benefits  for new job listings  at least six other states have similar  laws on their books while new york city  is the first city to move on this  at the same time states and cities are  making what used to be normal practices  illegal like employers asking job  candidates for their salary history and  punishing workers who decide to share  their pay information with others  companies are pushing back against this  shift to pay transparency one of their  arguments is that pay bans are often too  broad to be useful i think the important  piece is going to be really  digging into the demographics right how  do you disaggregate the data  within those bands and really look at  trends like really looking at the equity  implications of that so i think  starting even with general bands is a  step in the right direction they also  say it's bad timing because of how hard  it is to find workers right now and so  they need flexibility on pay  especially if their current staff is  paid less than the going rate  existing workers may leave if they don't  get a raise compounding the labor  shortage and adding to costs at a time  when companies are already trying to  manage rising inflation of course  workers too are facing inflation in  their everyday expenses as well  the other downside for employers sharing  pay details opens them up to legal risks  here's how  federal law prohibits them from fixing  wages but local paid transparency laws  may reveal the extent to which employers  sometimes share pay information with  each other even if they never actually  agreed to set wages  that could lead uncle sam to conclude  that a company violated anti-trust laws  no matter the efforts to reform pay and  equity there is one thing that is  outside the purview of the law  most women don't negotiate their pay  one survey finds 57 percent of women say  they never haggled over their salary  versus 51 percent of men  more data goes a long way but you don't  get what you don't ask for  [Music]
 

